<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Æthelwulf of Wessex</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 858</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Æthelwulf was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> from 839 to 858, the son of Ecgberht. He and his son Æthelbald won a great victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> at Aclea in 851, and in 855 he made a pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> with his youngest son Alfred, marrying the Frankish princess Judith on his return. Four of his sons, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a>, ruled Wessex after him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aethelwulf-of-wessex.jpg" alt="Æthelwulf of Wessex" width="320" height="293" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%C3%86thelwulf_-_MS_Royal_14_B_VI.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Æthelwulf was the son of Ecgberht, who was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> from 802 to 839, and of Osburh, daughter of Oslac, described by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asser">Asser</a> as King Æthelwulf's butler. In 825 Ecgberht defeated King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beornwulf-of-mercia">Beornwulf of Mercia</a> at the Battle of Ellandun in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wiltshire">Wiltshire</a>, ending a long Mercian ascendancy over southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. He then sent Æthelwulf with Eahlstan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sherborne">Sherborne</a>, and Wulfheard, Ealdorman of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hampshire">Hampshire</a>, and a large army into Kent to expel the Mercian sub-king Baldred. Æthelwulf became sub-king of Kent, with Surrey, Sussex and Essex, and governed there through Kentish ealdormen, advancing their interests, which won local support that Mercian rulers governing from a distance had lacked.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When he succeeded in 839 he was the first son to follow his father as West Saxon king since 641. He gave Kent and the south-east to his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelstan">Æthelstan</a> and governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> and Kent as separate spheres. Janet Nelson says he ran a Carolingian-style family firm of plural realms.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raids increased in the 840s: in 843 he was defeated by the crews of 35 ships at Carhampton in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somerset">Somerset</a>, and in 850 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelstan">Æthelstan</a> and Ealdorman Ealhhere won a naval victory off Sandwich. In 851 a fleet of 350 ships took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a> and defeated King Berhtwulf of Mercia, but Æthelwulf and his son Æthelbald defeated the Vikings at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-aclea">Battle of Aclea</a> in Surrey, after which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a> records the heathen army's heavy losses.</p><p>In 853 he joined a Mercian expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, and his daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelswith">Æthelswith</a> married King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgred-of-mercia">Burgred of Mercia</a>. In 855 he made a pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, first giving a decimation, a tenth of his property, and leaving Æthelbald to act as king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> and Æthelberht to rule Kent. He spent a year in Rome. On the way back he married Judith, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a>, in 856. Æthelbald refused to give up the West Saxon throne, and Æthelwulf agreed to divide the kingdom, taking the east.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Æthelwulf died on 13 January 858, leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> to Æthelbald and Kent to Æthelberht; Judith then married Æthelbald. Æthelbald died two years later and the kingdom was reunited. In the 20th century historians thought him excessively pious and impractical, and saw his pilgrimage as a desertion of duty. Historians now see a king who consolidated and extended his dynasty's power, commanded respect on the continent and dealt with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> more effectively than most of his contemporaries. His youngest son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a> laid on this base his own success. A ring inscribed Æthelwulf Rex, found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wiltshire">Wiltshire</a> about 1780, is now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Æthelwulf, King of Wessex” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Kingdom of Wessex</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.78,6.08&amp;year=848" aria-label="Aachen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4689 402 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Æthelwulf of Wessex"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5139 672h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5139 672h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.78,6.08&amp;year=848">What was here in 848 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
